@prefix courier: <http://example.org/orexis/courier#> .
@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix planning: <http://example.org/orexis/planning#> .

<> a planning:ShapesGraph .    # this document is one graph, and this is what it is

#  WHAT DELIVERED MEANS AND HOW FAR IT IS, the domain's: a world's desire points here with
#  `planning:metWhen` and `planning:estimates`. Delivered is the shape — on every parcel, the cell it
#  is at equals the cell it is owed at, so a carried parcel, at no cell, is not delivered — and the
#  estimate is what every parcel astray is still certainly owed in this domain's own actions —
#  the drives, its drop and, where it stands, its pick — which never overstates what they cost.
#
#  EACH BLOCK IS ABOUT THE PARCEL ITSELF, `planning:about sh:this`: the shape targets every parcel,
#  so under a DESIRE two parcels astray are two instances in trouble, and the derivation clusters a
#  witness per instance only where the block says what it is about. Saying nothing, the dispatcher's
#  two parcels were one want searched over both vans' every move — 674 candidates and 85 s for a
#  ten-step plan — where a want per parcel is found in 90 and under a second
#  (a-parcel-astray-is-a-want-of-its-own). A WANT pointing here, the courier's, reads it as before.

courier:delivered a sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:label "every parcel standing where it is owed" ;
    sh:targetClass courier:Parcel ;
    sh:property [ sh:path courier:at ; sh:equals courier:destination ; planning:about sh:this ] .

#  WHAT IS STILL OWED, PER PARCEL ASTRAY: aboard, the road to the door and the drop; standing
#  somewhere, the road to the parcel, its road home, the pick and the drop. Every action here
#  costs one, and each term is certain rather than guessed — a drive moves one cell, so the road
#  is the least the van drives; a parcel not at its door is dropped exactly once; one not yet
#  carried is picked exactly once — so the sum never overstates what is left. It counted the
#  drives alone until #898, which never overstated either, and was loose by one for a carried
#  parcel and two for a standing one, which is where a loose bound costs most: at the root, where
#  every world the frontier opens is judged against it. EACH BRANCH REPEATS THE PREAMBLE, since
#  a BIND inside a UNION branch cannot see a variable bound outside it — written the tidy way it
#  answered 0 for every world.
#
#  THE PARCEL IS `$this`, SHACL's word for the instance, and it stands where a variable and a
#  name are both legal — the subject of a pattern, the argument of a BIND — never in a projection
#  or a GROUP BY. Unbound, as the desire's and the courier's own want's, it is a variable and the
#  sum runs over every parcel astray; bound by the derivation to the one parcel a want is about,
#  the sum is that parcel's drives alone. Counting every parcel, a want per parcel read the other
#  van's drives in its `planning:remaining` too, and the other van's drive toward its own parcel
#  cost one, saved one and tied the frontier: 61 and 64 weighings against 13 for a van alone (#893).
courier:drivesOwed rdfs:label "drives still owed, and each parcel's pick and drop" ;
    sh:select """PREFIX courier: <http://example.org/orexis/courier#>
        SELECT (SUM(?d) AS ?estimate) WHERE {
            SELECT ?p (MIN(?dist) AS ?d) WHERE {
                {
                    $this a courier:Parcel ; courier:destination ?owed .
                    ?owed courier:x ?ox ; courier:y ?oy .
                    $this courier:carriedBy ?van . ?van courier:at ?vc .
                    ?vc courier:x ?vx ; courier:y ?vy .
                    BIND($this AS ?p)
                    BIND(ABS(?vx - ?ox) + ABS(?vy - ?oy) + 1 AS ?dist)
                } UNION {
                    $this a courier:Parcel ; courier:destination ?owed .
                    ?owed courier:x ?ox ; courier:y ?oy .
                    $this courier:at ?pc . ?v courier:at ?vc .
                    ?v a courier:Van .
                    ?pc courier:x ?px ; courier:y ?py .
                    ?vc courier:x ?vx ; courier:y ?vy .
                    FILTER(?px != ?ox || ?py != ?oy)
                    BIND($this AS ?p)
                    BIND(ABS(?vx - ?px) + ABS(?vy - ?py)
                         + ABS(?px - ?ox) + ABS(?py - ?oy) + 2 AS ?dist)
                }
            } GROUP BY ?p
        }""" .
